## ═══════════════════════════════════════

*Run through this before publishing every article.*

**SEO Checklist**
- [ ] **Tier plan:** 1 primary (Tier 2 or 3 for most guides), 3–6 secondary (Tier 3 + Tier 4 mix), ≤2 Tier‑1 touches unless the article is explicitly calculator/homepage-focused
- [ ] **No dump / no stuffing:** not repeating the same Tier‑1 phrase across sections; not pasting long example lists from the rule into the draft
- [ ] Primary keyword in headline (first 5 words if possible)
- [ ] Primary keyword in first paragraph
- [ ] Primary keyword in at least one H2 subheading
- [ ] Long-tail keywords used as subheadings or naturally in body copy
- [ ] Featured snippet questions answered directly and concisely
- [ ] Comparison table included (targets Google comparison snippets)
- [ ] Meta title written (50–60 characters)
- [ ] Meta description written (150–160 characters, includes keyword + click trigger)
- [ ] URL slug is short, keyword-rich, and hyphenated
- [ ] Internal links to other relevant articles (if available)
- [ ] External links to credible sources open in new tab

**Content Checklist**
- [ ] Opens with a hook that addresses the reader's frustration or goal
- [ ] Includes a clear definition section (answers "what is [topic]?")
- [ ] Science/research section includes minimum 2 cited sources
- [ ] Benefits section is honest — no overclaiming
- [ ] Risks section is included and thorough (builds credibility)
- [ ] Step-by-step how-to section is actionable and specific
- [ ] Primary website referenced and linked 4–8 times contextually
- [ ] Comparison table included
- [ ] Practical tips section included
- [ ] Strong, action-oriented conclusion with CTA
- [ ] Scientific references listed
- [ ] Medical/legal disclaimer included (for health topics)

**Readability Checklist**
- [ ] No paragraphs longer than 4–5 lines
- [ ] Mix of short punchy sentences and longer explanatory ones
- [ ] No filler phrases or weak openers
- [ ] Key terms bolded on first use
- [ ] Subheadings every 200–300 words to break up long sections
- [ ] Read aloud test: does it sound like a smart human wrote it?

